window.DATA_CATALOG=[{"id":"telco_churn","title":"Telecom customer churn (postpaid, India)","domain":"Telecom","level":"beginner","files":["telco_churn.csv"],"rows":6595,"summary":"One row per customer of a fictional Indian postpaid mobile and broadband operator, with plan, usage, billing and support history and a churn label for the next 90 days. It is the classic churn problem with the classic traps: numbers stored as text, leaky columns, duplicate customers, inconsistent spellings and blanks that carry meaning. About one customer in four churns, and a cleaned logistic regression reaches a ROC-AUC of about 0.80.","dictionary":[{"col":"customer_id","type":"str","meaning":"Customer key such as TC-100004. 95 rows repeat a customer; some copies are lower-case (tc-100004) or padded with spaces."},{"col":"signup_date","type":"str","meaning":"Date the customer joined, stored as text in four formats: 2018-05-28 (about 77%), 28/05/2018 (day first, 13%), 28-May-2018 (6%) and May 28, 2018 (4%)."},{"col":"city","type":"str","meaning":"City of the billing address. About 7% of values use another spelling (Bangalore, Bombay, Madras, mumbai, ...)."},{"col":"circle","type":"str","meaning":"Telecom circle (state-level licence area) that goes with the city. Blank for about 2%."},{"col":"gender","type":"str","meaning":"Male or Female as captured at activation. Blank for about 3%."},{"col":"age","type":"float","meaning":"Age in years at the snapshot date. Blank for about 2%; 18 customers hold a placeholder 0 and 6 hold impossible values above 100."},{"col":"married","type":"str","meaning":"Yes or No."},{"col":"dependents","type":"str","meaning":"Yes if the customer has dependents, otherwise No."},{"col":"tenure_months","type":"int","meaning":"Whole months between signup_date and the snapshot date 2025-03-31. About 0.5% of customers disagree with their signup_date by 9 to 24 months."},{"col":"contract","type":"str","meaning":"Commitment: Month-to-month, 1 year or 2 year. The file uses 10 spellings (Monthly, month-to-month, One year, 2 Year, ...)."},{"col":"plan_tier","type":"str","meaning":"Postpaid mobile plan: Basic 349, Standard 499, Premium 699 or Max 999 (rupees per month for the first line, before GST)."},{"col":"num_lines","type":"int","meaning":"Mobile lines on the account, 1 to 4."},{"col":"internet_service","type":"str","meaning":"Home broadband: Fibre, 5G Home (fixed wireless) or the text None. pandas reads None as a missing value."},{"col":"ott_bundle","type":"str","meaning":"Streaming bundle: Yes, No, or No internet service when the customer has no home broadband."},{"col":"device_protection","type":"str","meaning":"Yes or No."},{"col":"monthly_data_gb","type":"float","meaning":"Mobile data used in the last full month, in GB, with a long right tail. Blank for about 3%, mostly when usage was tiny; 13 customers hold the sentinel 9999."},{"col":"monthly_call_minutes","type":"int","meaning":"Outgoing call minutes in the last month. About 3% of customers made no calls."},{"col":"support_calls_90d","type":"int","meaning":"Calls to customer support in the last 90 days."},{"col":"network_tickets_90d","type":"int","meaning":"Network complaint tickets raised in the last 90 days."},{"col":"late_payments_6m","type":"int","meaning":"Number of bills paid after the due date in the last 6 months."},{"col":"payment_method","type":"str","meaning":"UPI AutoPay, Credit card auto-debit, NACH e-mandate, Net banking or Cash at store."},{"col":"paperless_billing","type":"str","meaning":"Yes if the bill is sent by email or app only, otherwise No."},{"col":"monthly_charges","type":"float","meaning":"Household monthly bill in rupees including 18% GST. 8 customers are 100 times too large (paise entered as rupees) and 6 are negative (credit notes)."},{"col":"total_charges","type":"str (numeric text)","meaning":"Lifetime billed amount in rupees, stored as text: a single space for customers with 0 months of tenure (77 rows), and thousands separators such as 1,234.50 on 83 rows."},{"col":"churn","type":"str","meaning":"TARGET. Yes if the customer disconnected or ported out within 90 days of the snapshot, otherwise No. About 3% of true churners are wrongly labelled No."},{"col":"cancellation_reason","type":"str","meaning":"LEAK. Reason code entered when the customer left. Blank for people who stayed (and for some who left)."},{"col":"winback_offer_sent","type":"str","meaning":"LEAK. Yes if the retention team sent a win-back offer. Mostly sent after the customer was already flagged as leaving."}],"gotchas":["Wrong dtypes and formats: total_charges is text (a single space for the 77 rows with 0 months of tenure, and thousands separators on 83 others), signup_date comes in four formats (in pandas 3.0 no single dayfirst setting of pd.to_datetime reads the whole column correctly, so parse each pattern separately), and the text None in internet_service is read by pandas as a missing value for about 48% of the rows.","Target leakage: cancellation_reason and winback_offer_sent are created after the customer leaves. A model that uses them looks brilliant (ROC-AUC near 0.98) and is useless in production.","Duplicates in two flavours: 70 exact copies from a double extract and 25 re-keyed copies whose customer_id is lower-case or padded with spaces, so drop_duplicates() alone leaves 25 of them behind.","Dirty values: city and contract have many spellings; age has placeholder 0 and impossible values over 100; monthly_data_gb has a 9999 sentinel; monthly_charges has paise-as-rupees and negative values; tenure_months disagrees with signup_date for about 0.5% of customers.","Missing values with three different mechanisms: gender and circle are blank completely at random, age is blank mostly for Cash at store customers (depends on another column), and monthly_data_gb is blank mostly when usage was tiny (depends on the missing value itself), so the blank is a useful signal.","Noisy and imbalanced label: only about one customer in four churns, and about 3% of true churners are labelled No because their port-out was not yet recorded. Some of them are visible because they still have a cancellation_reason."],"tasks":["Load the file with pandas and list the dtype and missing count of every column. Which columns are not the type you expect, and why does internet_service look almost half missing?","Convert total_charges to numbers. Show that the rows that fail are exactly the customers with tenure_months equal to 0, and decide what they should contain (0 or missing) and why.","Find the duplicates: first with drop_duplicates(), then after normalising customer_id with str.upper().str.strip(). How many real customers are there, and which copy of each would you keep?","Standardise the city and contract spellings, then chart churn rate by contract, tenure band and payment method. Write three sentences a manager could act on.","Investigate the missing data: test whether age is missing at random by comparing payment_method groups, and whether a blank monthly_data_gb carries information about churn. Choose an imputation plus a missing-indicator and justify it.","Make a stratified train/test split and fit a logistic regression baseline. Report ROC-AUC, PR-AUC and the confusion matrix. Then add cancellation_reason and winback_offer_sent as features, watch the AUC jump, and explain in two sentences why that model must not be used.","Compare logistic regression with gradient boosting using 5-fold cross-validation and a calibration plot. Choose the contact threshold from costs: a retention offer costs 300 rupees, and a customer saved is worth about 2,400 rupees of margin if 30% of contacted churners accept.","Interview question: your churn model scores 0.98 AUC offline but marketing says its list is useless. Name four causes you would check, in order, and show how each would appear in this dataset."],"answer_key":"SPOILER. Rows: 6,595 = 6,500 real customers + 70 exact duplicate rows + 25 re-keyed duplicates. After cleaning, 25.9% of the 6,500 customers churn (the true event rate is 26.1%; about 3% of true churners were labelled No and 0.7% of stayers were labelled Yes). Generating model (log-odds): month-to-month +0.95, 1 year -0.30, 2 year -1.40; tenure -0.035 per month up to 48 months; each support call +0.32 (cap 6), network ticket +0.20 (cap 5), late payment +0.40 (cap 4); Cash at store +0.60, UPI AutoPay -0.35, credit-card auto-debit -0.30, NACH -0.20, net banking +0.15; paperless billing +0.20; a bill that is high compared with data used +0.45 per standard deviation; Fibre without the OTT bundle +0.30, 5G Home +0.25; age under 26 +0.35, age 62 or more +0.25; married -0.30, dependents -0.20, -0.22 per extra line; unobserved noise with standard deviation 0.6. Observed churn rates after cleaning: month-to-month 40.8%, 1 year 14.3%, 2 year 6.6%; tenure 0 to 5 months 44.2% against 48 months or more 11.1%; Cash at store 39.8% against UPI AutoPay 21.8%. Paperless billing looks irrelevant in raw rates (26.2% for No against 25.6% for Yes) because paperless customers mostly pay by UPI or card, which lowers churn; its true effect (+0.20) appears once payment method is controlled for. Missing data: gender 3.1% and circle 2.1% are MCAR; age is blank for 8.8% of Cash at store customers against 1.4% of the others (MAR); monthly_data_gb is blank for 3.1% of customers, mostly tiny usage (MNAR), and those customers churn at 32.3% against 25.7% for the rest. Leakage: cancellation_reason is filled for 1,466 of the churners and for 49 customers labelled No (true churners whose label was not updated); winback_offer_sent is Yes for 83.2% of churners and 2.2% of the rest. Model results (stratified 25% hold-out): logistic regression on cleaned features about 0.80 ROC-AUC, gradient boosting about 0.79, the ceiling if you knew the true probabilities about 0.85, and about 0.98 once the two leak columns are added.","topics":["classification","churn","EDA","data cleaning","missing data","leakage","logistic regression","class imbalance","threshold selection"],"used_in":["study/analyst/ch05.html","study/analyst/ch07.html","study/ds/ch02.html","study/ds/ch03.html","study/ds/ch11.html","study/ml/ch04.html","study/ml/ch08.html","study/ml/ch09.html","study/ml/ch12.html","study/ds/ch15.html","project:3","project:7"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_telco_churn.py","file_info":[{"path":"telco_churn.csv","kb":1055}],"size_kb":1055,"columns":27},{"id":"ecom_store","title":"Online store: customers, orders, items, returns and clickstream (India)","domain":"E-commerce","level":"intermediate","files":["ecom_store/orders.csv","ecom_store/customers.csv","ecom_store/products.csv","ecom_store/order_items.csv","ecom_store/returns.csv","ecom_store/events.csv","ecom_store/ecom_store.db"],"rows":6000,"summary":"Six linked tables from a fictional Indian online store (2023-2024): 6,000 orders, 10,394 order items, 2,680 customer rows, 220 products, 796 returns and a 10,063-row clickstream, plus the same tables as a SQLite database. It is built for SQL joins, funnels, cohorts, RFM and data-quality work, and it contains migration problems you meet in real warehouses. Order timestamps switch from IST to UTC in March 2024, some customers exist twice, and a few keys point to nothing.","dictionary":[{"col":"orders.order_id","type":"int","meaning":"Order key (primary key)."},{"col":"orders.customer_id","type":"str","meaning":"Customer who placed the order. Orders of duplicated people are split across two ids."},{"col":"orders.order_ts","type":"str (datetime)","meaning":"Order time as text. Before 2024-03-01 it is IST like 2023-05-04 21:15:08+05:30; from 2024-03-01 it is UTC like 2024-05-04T15:45:08Z (48.5% of rows); 1.3% are manual dd/mm/yyyy hh:mm entries."},{"col":"orders.status","type":"str","meaning":"delivered, returned, cancelled or rto (return to origin). 11 different spellings because of capital letters."},{"col":"orders.payment_method","type":"str","meaning":"UPI, COD, Credit Card, Debit Card, Net Banking, Wallet or EMI."},{"col":"orders.ship_city","type":"str","meaning":"Delivery city."},{"col":"orders.coupon_code","type":"str","meaning":"Coupon used (blank if none)."},{"col":"orders.order_total","type":"str (numeric text)","meaning":"Items plus shipping in rupees, stored as text; 7.2% of rows look like ₹1,299.00 with the rupee sign and thousands separator."},{"col":"orders.shipping_fee","type":"float","meaning":"0 for baskets of 499 rupees or more, otherwise 49."},{"col":"orders.delivered_date","type":"str (date)","meaning":"Delivery date. Blank for cancelled and rto orders (structurally missing)."},{"col":"orders.delivery_rating","type":"int","meaning":"Customer rating 1 to 5. Blank for 58% of delivered orders, and the missingness depends on the rating itself."},{"col":"customers.customer_id","type":"str","meaning":"Customer key: C followed by digits, or U- followed by digits for the duplicate rows created by the app migration."},{"col":"customers.email_hash","type":"str","meaning":"Hashed email. 80 hashes appear twice: the same person has two customer rows."},{"col":"customers.signup_ts","type":"str (datetime)","meaning":"Signup time as text: IST with +05:30 before 2024-03-01, UTC with Z afterwards."},{"col":"customers.city","type":"str","meaning":"Home city."},{"col":"customers.state","type":"str","meaning":"Home state."},{"col":"customers.pincode","type":"int","meaning":"Six-digit postal code (synthetic)."},{"col":"customers.acquisition_channel","type":"str","meaning":"Organic, Paid social, Google ads, Referral or Email."},{"col":"customers.birth_year","type":"int","meaning":"Year of birth. Blank for 9%, more often for customers who pay by COD (depends on another column)."},{"col":"customers.gender","type":"str","meaning":"Female or Male; blank for about 3%."},{"col":"customers.loyalty_tier","type":"str","meaning":"Basic, Silver or Gold."},{"col":"products.product_id","type":"int","meaning":"Product key. Order items with product_id 9999 point to no product."},{"col":"products.sku","type":"str","meaning":"Stock keeping unit code."},{"col":"products.product_name","type":"str","meaning":"Product name."},{"col":"products.category","type":"str","meaning":"Electronics, Fashion, Home & Kitchen, Beauty or Grocery & Staples."},{"col":"products.brand","type":"str","meaning":"Brand name; blank for about 4%."},{"col":"products.mrp","type":"int","meaning":"Maximum retail price in rupees (GST inclusive)."},{"col":"products.cost_price","type":"float","meaning":"Purchase cost per unit in rupees."},{"col":"products.gst_rate","type":"int","meaning":"GST rate in percent (5, 12 or 18) as applicable in 2023-24."},{"col":"products.launch_date","type":"str (date)","meaning":"ISO launch date."},{"col":"products.is_active","type":"int","meaning":"1 if still sold."},{"col":"order_items.order_item_id","type":"int","meaning":"Line key."},{"col":"order_items.order_id","type":"int","meaning":"Parent order."},{"col":"order_items.product_id","type":"int","meaning":"Product bought. 25 rows have product_id 9999 that does not exist in products."},{"col":"order_items.quantity","type":"int","meaning":"Units bought. 12 rows are 0 or -1 (data entry errors)."},{"col":"order_items.unit_price","type":"float","meaning":"Selling price per unit in rupees. 163 rows from a legacy batch are 100 times too large (paise)."},{"col":"order_items.discount_pct","type":"int","meaning":"Discount percent applied (higher in the sale weeks)."},{"col":"order_items.gst_amount","type":"float","meaning":"GST included in the line total."},{"col":"order_items.line_total","type":"float","meaning":"unit_price x quantity (before the legacy paise batch was applied, so it stays correct for those rows)."},{"col":"returns.return_id","type":"int","meaning":"Return key."},{"col":"returns.order_item_id","type":"int","meaning":"Returned line. 8 rows point to an item that does not exist."},{"col":"returns.return_ts","type":"str (datetime)","meaning":"Return time as IST text without an offset."},{"col":"returns.reason","type":"str","meaning":"Size/fit issue, Damaged on arrival, Not as described, Changed mind or Wrong item."},{"col":"returns.refund_amount","type":"float","meaning":"Refund in rupees."},{"col":"events.event_id","type":"int","meaning":"Event key."},{"col":"events.session_id","type":"str","meaning":"Session key such as S800012."},{"col":"events.customer_id","type":"str","meaning":"Customer if logged in; blank for anonymous sessions."},{"col":"events.event_ts","type":"str (datetime)","meaning":"Event time as text with the same IST/UTC switch as orders."},{"col":"events.event_type","type":"str","meaning":"page_view, product_view, add_to_cart, begin_checkout or purchase."},{"col":"events.product_id","type":"int","meaning":"Product involved (blank for page views)."},{"col":"events.device","type":"str","meaning":"mobile or desktop."},{"col":"events.traffic_source","type":"str","meaning":"organic_search, direct, email or paid_social."}],"gotchas":["Duplicated customers after a migration: 80 people have a second customers row (id starting U-) with the same email_hash, and 105 orders were moved to the second id. Counting customer_id gives 2,680 customers but there are only 2,600 real people, so repeat rate and lifetime value are wrong until you deduplicate on email_hash.","Time zones and formats: orders from 2024-03-01 are UTC with a Z suffix (48.5% of orders) while earlier ones are IST, and 1.3% are manual dd/mm/yyyy entries. Grouping text dates directly puts evening orders on the wrong day and moves the busiest hour by 5.5 hours.","Mix effect in the clickstream (a Simpson-style trap): overall, mobile sessions convert at 5.3% against 6.8% for desktop, yet inside organic search (6.8% against 6.1%), direct (13.2% against 8.2%) and email (15.0% against 7.1%) mobile converts better. Mobile traffic is 55% paid social, which converts poorly; desktop has only 8% (too few paid_social desktop sessions to read reliably). The events table is a sampled clickstream and is not linked to orders.","Money and key problems: 7.2% of order_total values are text with the rupee sign, 163 order items are in paise, 25 items reference a missing product, 8 returns reference a missing item and 12 items have quantity 0 or -1.","Missing data with meaning: delivery_rating is blank for 58% of delivered orders and customers rate mostly when very happy or very unhappy (MNAR), so the average of 3.69 is biased against the true mean of 3.64. birth_year is blank more often for COD customers (MAR)."],"tasks":["Open ecom_store.db in SQLite and list each table's row count. Check primary keys and find the orphan rows with LEFT JOIN ... WHERE ... IS NULL (items without product, returns without item).","Clean order_ts: parse IST and UTC rows, convert everything to IST, and compare daily orders before and after the clean-up. Which hours change most?","Deduplicate customers on email_hash, build a mapping from every customer_id to a person_id, and recompute customers, repeat-purchase rate and average orders per person.","SQL: monthly revenue, orders and average order value from order_items, excluding cancelled and rto orders; then month-over-month growth with a window function. Mention the paise rows.","SQL: cohort retention by signup month (share of each cohort that orders again in months 1, 2, 3) after deduplication.","SQL: build an RFM table on delivered orders, bucket into quartiles and name the top segment. Compare the result with and without person-level deduplication.","From events, compute the funnel page_view to purchase by device and by traffic source. Explain why mobile looks worse overall but better inside three of the four sources.","Model whether an order will be cancelled or returned using payment_method, city tier, discount and basket value, and check the effect of COD. Explain why delivery_rating must not be used."],"answer_key":"SPOILER. Tables: orders 6,000, customers 2,680 (2,600 real people + 80 duplicate rows), products 220, order_items 10,394, returns 796, events 10,063 (3,000 sessions). 105 orders were reassigned to the duplicate ids (45% of each duplicated person's orders). Orders switch from IST to UTC on 2024-03-01 (48.5% Z rows); manual dd/mm/yyyy rows 1.3%. Demand: growth of about 90% over two years, weekday pattern (Saturday 1.15, Sunday 1.10, Monday 0.95), Diwali ramp (multiplier 1.9 on 2023-11-12 and 2.0 on 2024-11-01 across the 8 days before), and two invented Mega Saver Days weeks (2023-10-15 to 21 and 2024-10-04 to 10) with a 1.7 and 1.8 multiplier; orders per day in the 2023 sale week were 1.9 times the previous four weeks. Cancellation: COD 9.6% against 4.0% prepaid; RTO only on COD (6.1% against 0.0%). Return rate per delivered item: Fashion 17.4%, Grocery & Staples 1.0% (generating base rates Fashion 16%, Electronics 5%, Home 6%, Beauty 4%, Grocery 1%, plus COD and discount effects). Ratings: true rating is observed with probability 0.75 for 1, 2 and 5 stars and 0.22 for 3 and 4 stars; observed mean 3.69 against true mean 3.64. Events: generating session-to-purchase rates per source (desktop) organic 6%, direct 9%, email 12%, paid_social 1.5%, with mobile 1.25 times better inside every source; the paid_social desktop sample is too small to show it. Planted counts: 163 paise items, 25 orphan items, 8 orphan returns, 12 zero/negative quantities.","topics":["SQL","joins","data cleaning","deduplication","time zones","funnel","cohort retention","RFM","data quality"],"used_in":["study/sql/ch04.html","study/sql/ch06.html","study/sql/ch08.html","study/sql/ch12.html","study/sql/ch15.html","study/analyst/ch05.html","study/analyst/ch12.html","study/de/ch05.html","study/ds/ch04.html","study/python/ch05.html","project:13","project:14","project:15","project:18","project:21","project:62"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_ecom_store.py","file_info":[{"path":"ecom_store/orders.csv","kb":521},{"path":"ecom_store/customers.csv","kb":250},{"path":"ecom_store/products.csv","kb":20},{"path":"ecom_store/order_items.csv","kb":450},{"path":"ecom_store/returns.csv","kb":44},{"path":"ecom_store/events.csv","kb":720},{"path":"ecom_store/ecom_store.db","kb":2456}],"size_kb":4460,"columns":11},{"id":"retail_demand","title":"Daily store x SKU sales for an Indian supermarket chain","domain":"Retail / Supply chain","level":"intermediate","files":["retail_demand.csv"],"rows":21786,"summary":"Daily unit sales for 6 products in 5 stores of a fictional Indian supermarket chain over 731 days (2023-2024), with promotions, closing stock, festivals and temperature. Sales are what was sold, not what customers wanted: stockouts hide demand. Festival dates move between years, one store exports dates day-first, and a few rows are missing or broken.","dictionary":[{"col":"date","type":"str (date)","meaning":"Sales date as text. Store S4 (Kolkata, 4,372 rows) uses dd/mm/yyyy; all other stores use ISO yyyy-mm-dd, so days 1 to 12 are ambiguous."},{"col":"store_id","type":"str","meaning":"Store code S1 to S5."},{"col":"store_city","type":"str","meaning":"Mumbai (S1), Bengaluru (S2), Delhi (S3), Kolkata (S4), Chennai (S5). About 2% of rows use another spelling (Bombay, Bangalore, Calcutta, uppercase)."},{"col":"sku_id","type":"str","meaning":"Product code SKU01 to SKU06."},{"col":"sku_name","type":"str","meaning":"Packaged Milk 1L, Cold Drink 2L, Dry Fruit Gift Box, Umbrella, Instant Noodles 4-pack, Olive Oil 500ml."},{"col":"units_sold","type":"int","meaning":"TARGET. Units sold that day. Capped by available stock (censored demand). 12 rows are negative (returns netted)."},{"col":"unit_price","type":"int","meaning":"List price in rupees. 8 rows are 0 and 5 rows are 100 times too large (paise)."},{"col":"promo_flag","type":"int","meaning":"1 on promotion days."},{"col":"discount_pct","type":"int","meaning":"Discount in percent on promotion days (0 otherwise)."},{"col":"closing_stock","type":"int","meaning":"LEAK for forecasting. Units left at the end of the day, known only after the day is over. 0 means a stockout occurred or stock exactly ran out."},{"col":"store_open","type":"int","meaning":"0 when the store was closed (84 rows: store S2 renovation), else 1."},{"col":"festival","type":"str","meaning":"Name of the festival on that date (blank on other days). Only the festival day itself is marked, not the build-up."},{"col":"temp_c","type":"float","meaning":"Daily temperature in degrees Celsius."},{"col":"revenue_inr","type":"float","meaning":"LEAK. units_sold x unit_price x (1 - discount_pct/100). It matches this formula on 99.90% of rows."}],"gotchas":["Censored demand: sales equal demand only when stock is available. About 6.2% of store-SKU-days were stockouts, losing 5.4% of true demand, concentrated around Diwali and other peaks. Zero or capped sales are not zero demand; closing_stock of 0 flags many of them (7.3% of rows).","Mixed date formats: store S4 writes dd/mm/yyyy (4,372 rows) and the rest write ISO. Parsing everything with one format silently swaps day and month on S4 for days 1 to 12.","Missing and non-demand rows: a 3-day feed outage (2023-06-14 to 2023-06-16) is missing for all stores, store S3 has a 14-day gap (2024-02-05 to 2024-02-18), and store S2 was closed for 14 days (84 rows with store_open 0 and zero sales).","Leakage and bad values: revenue_inr and closing_stock are computed from the target or known after the day; 30 duplicate rows, 8 zero prices, 5 prices in paise, 12 negative units.","Moving festivals and confounded promotions: Diwali fell on 2023-11-12 and 2024-11-01 (Holi, Eid and others also move), so day-of-year seasonality cannot capture them; promotions are more frequent in the summer, which confounds the promo lift for cold drinks."],"tasks":["Parse the dates correctly (S4 is day first), remove the duplicate rows and list every missing store-day. Which gaps are outages and which are closures?","Plot weekly units for each SKU. Describe trend, weekday pattern and season for each product, and check how Diwali shows up in 2023 and 2024.","Fix the bad values (zero price, paise prices, negative units) and explain your rule for each.","Build naive and seasonal-naive (7-day) forecasts for the last 8 weeks and report MAE and MASE per SKU.","Add features (weekday, festival window, promo, lag 7, lag 14, rolling 28-day mean) and fit a gradient boosting or ridge model with a rolling-origin backtest. Show which leak columns you must exclude.","Estimate the promotion lift for Instant Noodles with log(units) on log(1 - discount) and controls. Compare with the generating elasticity in the answer key.","Use closing_stock to flag censored days, then estimate how many units were lost for Dry Fruit Gift Box around Diwali, and propose a stocking rule.","Interview question: your model has 5% error in backtests but the supply planner says it under-forecasts every Diwali. What do you check?"],"answer_key":"SPOILER. Demand per store-SKU-day = base x store scale x weekday x season x festival x promotion x trend x noise (gamma-Poisson). Weekday multipliers (Monday first): 0.92, 0.90, 0.93, 0.96, 1.05, 1.22, 1.18, so Saturday/Monday is 1.33 (measured on milk: 1.32). Festival peaks (true demand multipliers on the festival day, with a ramp before it): Dry Fruit Gift Box 3.4 for Diwali (12-day ramp), 1.8 Raksha Bandhan, 1.6 Christmas, 1.3 Dussehra; Cold Drink 1.5 Holi, 1.2 Eid; Durga Puja lifts store S4 only. Measured in S1 dry fruit: units in the week up to Diwali 2023 were 2.0 times the first two weeks of October. Promotion price elasticity is -2.5 (lift = (1 - discount)^-2.5), followed by a 5-day post-promo dip of 15%. Cold Drink seasonality peaks in May, Umbrella in July (monsoon). Trend: +12% over two years. Stockouts: a base-stock rule that ignores festivals censors 6.2% of store-SKU-days and 5.4% of true demand. Rows: 21,786 = full grid minus the outage and the S3 gap, plus 30 duplicates. Revenue leak: revenue_inr matches units x price x (1 - discount) on 99.90% of rows (22 mismatches caused by the planted price and unit errors).","topics":["time series","forecasting","seasonality","censored demand","backtesting","feature engineering","leakage","data cleaning"],"used_in":["study/ds/ch09.html","study/ds/ch11.html","study/ds/ch12.html","study/ds/ch15.html","study/stats/ch10.html","study/analyst/ch05.html","study/ml/ch08.html","project:26","project:68","project:56"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_retail_demand.py","file_info":[{"path":"retail_demand.csv","kb":1544}],"size_kb":1544,"columns":14},{"id":"credit_loans","title":"Personal-loan applications and defaults (Indian digital lender)","domain":"Banking / Lending","level":"advanced","files":["credit_loans.csv"],"rows":10790,"summary":"10,750 unsecured personal-loan applications to a fictional Indian digital lender over 24 months, with applicant, bureau and loan features, the approval decision and a 12-month default flag. Defaults are only known for approved loans, repeat applicants appear several times, and some columns are only known after the loan is booked. It is built for scorecards, validation design and reject inference.","dictionary":[{"col":"application_id","type":"str","meaning":"Unique application key such as LA9000012 (40 rows are exact duplicates of another row)."},{"col":"applicant_id","type":"str","meaning":"Person key. 1,500 applications (14.0%) belong to an applicant who applied more than once, mostly after a rejection."},{"col":"application_date","type":"str (date)","meaning":"Date applied. ISO text, but 418 rows use dd/mm/yyyy."},{"col":"age","type":"int","meaning":"Applicant age in years. Blank for about 1%; 10 impossible values (for example 5 or 150)."},{"col":"city_tier","type":"str","meaning":"City tier. 452 rows use another spelling (T1, tier 2, TIER-3)."},{"col":"employment_type","type":"str","meaning":"Salaried, Self-employed, Gig/Platform or Government."},{"col":"monthly_income_inr","type":"str (numeric text)","meaning":"Self-reported monthly income in rupees, heaped at round numbers. 248 rows are text such as 45K, 1.2L or 45,000."},{"col":"existing_emi_inr","type":"int","meaning":"Monthly instalments the applicant already pays on other loans (0 if none)."},{"col":"foir","type":"float","meaning":"Fixed obligations to income ratio including the new loan's instalment."},{"col":"bureau_score","type":"int","meaning":"Credit bureau score on a 300 to 900 scale. Blank for 13.8% of applications (thin file: new to credit)."},{"col":"enquiries_6m","type":"int","meaning":"Credit enquiries in the last 6 months."},{"col":"delinquencies_24m","type":"int","meaning":"Missed payments in the last 24 months."},{"col":"credit_utilisation","type":"float","meaning":"Share of revolving credit limit used (can exceed 1)."},{"col":"loan_amount_inr","type":"int","meaning":"Requested loan amount in rupees."},{"col":"tenure_months","type":"int","meaning":"Requested tenure: 12, 18, 24, 36, 48 or 60 months."},{"col":"interest_rate_pct","type":"float","meaning":"Annual interest rate offered. Set by the lender's pricing from risk, so it partly reveals the lender's own view."},{"col":"purpose","type":"str","meaning":"Stated purpose of the loan."},{"col":"channel","type":"str","meaning":"Digital, DSA agent or Branch. Digital share grows over time (population drift)."},{"col":"approved","type":"int","meaning":"1 if the application was approved and booked, else 0 (61.4% approved)."},{"col":"default_12m","type":"int","meaning":"TARGET. 1 if the loan defaulted within 12 months. Blank for all 4,147 rejected applications (outcome unknown)."},{"col":"first_emi_bounced","type":"int","meaning":"LEAK. 1 if the first instalment bounced. Known only after booking. Blank if not approved."},{"col":"days_past_due_max_6m","type":"int","meaning":"LEAK. Worst days past due in the first 6 months after booking. Blank if not approved."},{"col":"collections_calls","type":"int","meaning":"LEAK. Collection calls made after booking. Blank if not approved."}],"gotchas":["Reject-inference bias: default_12m exists only for the 6,603 approved loans (observed default rate 8.0%). Rejected applicants (blank outcome) would have defaulted at about 22.5%, so a model trained on approved loans never sees the risky end of the population.","Target leakage: first_emi_bounced, days_past_due_max_6m and collections_calls are recorded after booking (correlation with default 0.52, 0.89 and 0.89). interest_rate_pct also carries the lender's own risk view.","Repeat applicants: 14.0% of applications come from people who applied more than once, so a random train/test split puts the same person on both sides. Split by applicant_id.","Drift and thin files: gig-worker defaults rise from 15.2% before 2024 to 21.8% from January 2024 (other employment types 7.2% to 7.0%), and the digital channel share grows. 13.8% of applications have no bureau score and default far more (21.7% against 6.5% among approved).","Dirty fields: 40 duplicate rows, mixed date formats, income as text (45K, 1.2L, 45,000), tier spelling variants, impossible ages, income heaped at round numbers, and 11 true defaults recorded as paid by a reporting lag (label noise)."],"tasks":["Load the file, fix dtypes (dates, income text such as 45K and 1.2L), drop exact duplicates and profile every column. What share of default_12m is blank and why?","Plot default rate by bureau-score band, by employment type and by application quarter. What changes in 2024?","Bin the main features with weight of evidence (WoE), compute information value (IV), and drop weak features. Treat the missing bureau score as its own bin.","Fit a logistic scorecard on approved loans only with a time-based split. Report AUC, Gini and KS in-time and out-of-time, and compute PSI for income and employment mix between early and late applications.","Add first_emi_bounced and days_past_due_max_6m to the model and watch the metrics jump. Explain why this model cannot be used at the moment of decision.","Compare a random 5-fold split with GroupKFold on applicant_id. How much does the score change, and why?","Reject inference: score the rejected applicants with your model, compare the predicted risk with the approved group, and discuss what extrapolation or parceling would assume.","Interview question: your scorecard has Gini 0.55 in validation but the live default rate is twice the forecast. List four likely causes from this dataset and how you would test each."],"answer_key":"SPOILER. 10,750 unique applications (40 duplicate rows added) from 10,000 people. 61.4% approved by a policy (score at least 650, thin-file rule, FOIR below 0.55, income at least 18,000, 3-4% random overrides). Observed default rate among approved: 8.0%; the true rate is 8.1% before 11 defaults were mislabelled as paid. If the rejected applicants had been approved, about 22.5% would have defaulted (generator probabilities). Default log-odds drivers: bureau score (-0.011 per point), FOIR (+2.2), utilisation (+0.8), enquiries (+0.14 each), delinquencies (+0.45 each), log income (-0.30), self-employed +0.25, gig +0.40, government -0.35, DSA channel +0.30, thin file +0.5, age under 25 +0.25, plus a +0.7 stress effect for gig workers from 2024-01-01. Observed: thin-file default 21.7% against 6.5%; gig default 15.2% before 2024 and 21.8% after; the lowest bureau score among approved loans is 473. Leak correlations with default: first_emi_bounced 0.52, days_past_due_max_6m 0.89, collections_calls 0.89. 14.0% of applications are repeats, so group-aware validation is needed.","topics":["credit risk","scorecard","WoE and IV","reject inference","leakage","validation design","population drift","class imbalance"],"used_in":["study/ds/ch11.html","study/ds/ch12.html","study/ds/ch16.html","study/ml/ch08.html","study/ml/ch09.html","study/ml/ch12.html","study/ml/ch16.html","study/stats/ch08.html","study/mlops/ch10.html","project:28","project:42","project:56"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_credit_loans.py","file_info":[{"path":"credit_loans.csv","kb":1300}],"size_kb":1300,"columns":23},{"id":"ab_checkout","title":"A/B test: one-page checkout redesign (shopping app)","domain":"E-commerce / Experimentation","level":"intermediate","files":["ab_checkout.csv"],"rows":14348,"summary":"One row per user assigned in a 21-day test of a new one-page checkout against the old three-step checkout in an Indian shopping app. The raw result looks like a clear win, but the data hides a sample-ratio mismatch, a novelty effect, a few giant bulk orders and a handful of users who saw both versions. Use it to practise the full checklist: validate the split, then analyse conversion and revenue honestly.","dictionary":[{"col":"user_id","type":"str","meaning":"User key such as U20000012. 170 users appear twice, once in each arm (cookie reset)."},{"col":"assign_ts","type":"str (datetime)","meaning":"Time of assignment as ISO text. Most rows are IST with +05:30; about 3.2% are UTC with a Z suffix (logging bug), so they look 5.5 hours earlier."},{"col":"variant","type":"str","meaning":"control (old checkout) or treatment (new one-page checkout)."},{"col":"device_class","type":"str","meaning":"Android mid/high, Android low-end, iOS or Web. Blank for about 1.0%."},{"col":"new_user","type":"int","meaning":"1 if the user had never ordered before, else 0 (45% are new)."},{"col":"city_tier","type":"str","meaning":"Tier 1, Tier 2 or Tier 3 city. Blank for about 2.9%."},{"col":"traffic_source","type":"str","meaning":"organic, paid_social, paid_search, email, direct, or unknown (unknown is concentrated on Web: 23% of Web rows)."},{"col":"pre_orders_30d","type":"int","meaning":"Orders placed in the 30 days before the test (0 for new users). Usable as a pre-experiment covariate."},{"col":"pre_spend_30d","type":"int","meaning":"Rupees spent in the 30 days before the test (0 for new users)."},{"col":"converted","type":"int","meaning":"PRIMARY METRIC. 1 if the user completed a purchase during the test, else 0."},{"col":"revenue_inr","type":"int","meaning":"Rupees of revenue from the purchase (0 if not converted). Heavy right tail: 9 bulk orders above 100,000 rupees."},{"col":"payment_error","type":"int","meaning":"GUARDRAIL METRIC. 1 if the user hit a payment error, else 0."}],"gotchas":["Sample-ratio mismatch: the test was meant to be 50/50 but control has 7,647 rows and treatment 6,701 (treatment share 46.7%, chi-square p = 2.8e-15). A redirect bug silently dropped low-end Android users from treatment (low-end Android is 19.3% of control but 10.0% of treatment), so the two arms are not comparable and the raw lift is biased upward.","Novelty effect: the observed lift is 4.5 percentage points in week 1 but only 1.4 percentage points and 1.4 percentage points in weeks 2 and 3. A single pooled number over 21 days overstates what will last.","Heavy-tailed revenue: 9 bulk orders above 100,000 rupees make up 59% of all revenue, so a mean-revenue t-test is unstable. Use medians, trimming, winsorising or a bootstrap.","Contaminated users and time zones: 170 users appear in both arms, and about 3.2% of timestamps are UTC instead of IST, which moves some assignments to the wrong day.","Missing and weak covariates: device_class is blank for about 1.0% and city_tier for about 2.9%, and the pre-period spend barely predicts conversion (correlation 0.05), so CUPED helps very little here."],"tasks":["Load the data, parse assign_ts (two formats), and count users per variant per day. Plot the daily split.","Test the 50/50 assignment with a chi-square (SRM) test. What does the p-value tell you, and should you trust the rest of the analysis yet?","Find the cause of the SRM: compare device_class, new_user and traffic_source mix between arms. Which segment is missing from treatment?","Compute the conversion lift with a two-proportion z-test and a 95% interval, first on all users, then after removing the segment that breaks the split from both arms. How much does the answer change?","Plot the lift by day or week and describe the shape. Decide what lift you would expect to keep after the novelty fades, and what you would report to the product manager.","Analyse revenue per user: show why the mean is unstable, then use a log transform, trimmed mean or bootstrap. Check the payment_error guardrail too.","Try variance reduction with pre_spend_30d (CUPED) and report how much the standard error shrinks, and why it is so small here.","Interview question: your A/B test shows a significant +3.5 point lift but the SRM check fails. Walk through what you do next and what you tell the stakeholder."],"answer_key":"SPOILER. Design: 15,000 users were randomised 50/50 (control or treatment). Treatment users on low-end Android were dropped with probability 0.55 by a redirect bug (822 users), so treatment has a richer device mix. Observed: control 14.5%, treatment 18.0% (lift 3.5 percentage points); SRM p = 2.8e-15. True effect of the new checkout (log-odds on conversion): +0.80 for new users and +0.25 for returning users on day 0, decaying as exp(-day/4), so it is about zero by day 14. In probability terms the true lift for users randomised to treatment was 3.2 percentage points in week 1, 0.5 percentage points in week 2 and 0.1 percentage points in week 3. Observed lift by week (all users): 4.5 percentage points, 1.4 percentage points, 1.4 percentage points; part of the later-week lift is the SRM selection bias, not the redesign. Revenue per converter is lognormal (median about 1,100 rupees) plus 9 bulk orders of 150,000 to 900,000 rupees (59% of revenue). Guardrail: payment_error is 4.4% in control and 5.4% in treatment (true +0.7 point harm). The right conclusion: do not ship on the pooled lift; fix the redirect bug, rerun, and expect a small long-run effect.","topics":["A/B testing","SRM","novelty effect","hypothesis testing","confidence intervals","heavy tails","CUPED","guardrail metrics"],"used_in":["study/analyst/ch13.html","study/ds/ch07.html","study/ds/ch04.html","study/stats/ch04.html","study/stats/ch05.html","study/stats/ch12.html","study/ds/ch15.html","project:3","project:25","project:67","project:69"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_ab_checkout.py","file_info":[{"path":"ab_checkout.csv","kb":1213}],"size_kb":1213,"columns":12},{"id":"house_prices","title":"Indian city housing listings (asking prices)","domain":"Real estate","level":"intermediate","files":["house_prices/listings.csv","house_prices/localities.csv"],"rows":4962,"rows_by_file":{"listings.csv":4962,"localities.csv":96},"summary":"About 5,000 asking-price listings from a fictional property portal in eight Indian cities, posted in 2023 and 2024, plus a small locality table. Prices arrive as text in lakh and crore style, areas come in three definitions and three units, and the same flat is often listed by several brokers. Use it to practise cleaning, honest regression and leakage-safe validation; locality names are real places but every price, distance and listing is synthetic.","dictionary":[{"file":"listings.csv","col":"listing_id","type":"id","meaning":"Portal listing id (HP-xxxxxx). The exact duplicate rows repeat the same id."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"listing_date","type":"datetime","meaning":"Date the listing was posted, 2023-01-01 to 2024-12-31. Stored as text in three formats (2023-05-12, 12/05/2023, 12 May 2023)."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"city","type":"category","meaning":"City as typed by the poster. Several spellings per city (Bangalore and Bengaluru, Bombay and Mumbai, extra spaces, lower case)."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"locality","type":"string","meaning":"Neighbourhood as typed by the poster. Case, spaces and a city suffix vary. Joins to localities.csv after cleaning."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"property_type","type":"category","meaning":"Kind of property. Spelling varies (Flat, Apt, House ...). Clean values: Apartment, Builder Floor, Independent House, Villa, Studio."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"bhk","type":"string","meaning":"Bedroom count as free text ('3 BHK', '3BHK', '3', '3.0', '1 RK', 'Studio'). Parse the number."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"bathrooms","type":"float","meaning":"Number of bathrooms. Some are missing and a few are impossible."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"area","type":"float","meaning":"Stated area as a number. Its unit is in area_unit and its definition is in area_type. A few values are impossible (0, 5, 99999)."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"area_unit","type":"category","meaning":"Unit of area: sqft (square feet), sqyd (square yards) or sqm (square metres)."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"area_type","type":"category","meaning":"Which area concept the number means: Carpet Area, Built-up Area or Super built-up Area."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"price","type":"string","meaning":"Asking price in INR written as text in many styles ('1.25 Cr', '85 Lac', '85L', 'Rs 1,25,00,000', '12500000'), or 'Price on request' / 'Contact for price'."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"price_per_sqft","type":"float","meaning":"Price per square foot printed by the portal, INR per sqft. Blank for some rows and inconsistent with the other columns for some."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"floor","type":"float","meaning":"Floor of the flat (0 = ground). Blank for houses and villas, and for some flats. A few values are above total_floors."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"total_floors","type":"float","meaning":"Number of floors in the building. Blank for houses and villas."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"age_years","type":"float","meaning":"Age of the building in years. Blank when the home is under construction or the poster left it out. A few values are impossible (-2, 150)."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"possession_status","type":"category","meaning":"Ready to move, or Under construction."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"furnishing","type":"category","meaning":"Furnishing level written in several spellings (Unfurnished, Semi-Furnished, Furnished ...). Often blank for builder listings."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"parking","type":"int","meaning":"Number of parking slots, 0 to 2."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"lift","type":"category","meaning":"Lift in the building, written as Yes, No, 1 or 0."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"amenities_count","type":"int","meaning":"Number of listed amenities (gym, pool, club house ...)."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"facing","type":"category","meaning":"Direction the home faces (East, North-East ...)."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"rera_registered","type":"category","meaning":"Whether the project is registered with the state real-estate regulator (RERA): Yes, No or blank."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"posted_by","type":"category","meaning":"Who posted the listing: Owner, Broker or Builder."},{"file":"listings.csv","col":"metro_km","type":"float","meaning":"Synthetic distance to the nearest metro or suburban rail station in km. Blank for some rows."},{"file":"localities.csv","col":"city","type":"category","meaning":"City name in clean spelling."},{"file":"localities.csv","col":"locality","type":"string","meaning":"Neighbourhood name in clean spelling (real place names, synthetic attributes)."},{"file":"localities.csv","col":"tier","type":"category","meaning":"Price tier of the locality: A prime, B good, C middle, D budget or outskirts."},{"file":"localities.csv","col":"km_to_cbd","type":"float","meaning":"Rough synthetic distance to the city's main business district in km."}],"gotchas":["Price is text in lakh and crore notation with several spellings. Some rows say 'Price on request', and a few prices have the wrong order of magnitude.","Area comes in three definitions (carpet, built-up, super built-up) and three units, so rows are not comparable until you convert them. A few areas are impossible.","The same flat is often posted several times by different brokers with slightly different price and area, and a few rows are exact copies. Random train/test splits leak these twins.","BHK, bathrooms and area move together. Coefficients change size and even sign when you add or drop columns, and the spread of raw rupee prices grows with the price.","Missing values come from different mechanisms: some are structural (floors for houses), and some depend on the hidden value itself (expensive homes hide the price, old buildings hide their age).","The price is an asking price. Who posts it, the listing date and the mix of old and new stock across localities all shift it, so a raw comparison hides the real effects."],"tasks":["Read both files. Clean the city and locality spellings and join localities.csv. Report how many listings fail to match (it should be only a few).","Write a function that turns price into rupees. Count the 'Price on request' rows and the rows you cannot parse, then print the median price by city in crore.","Convert every area to carpet-area square feet using the ratios in the answer key. Compare price per carpet sqft by city with the portal's own price_per_sqft column.","Find impossible values (tiny or huge areas, floor above total floors, ages below 0 or above 100, 9 bathrooms in a small flat) and prices with the wrong magnitude. Decide for each whether to fix, blank or drop it, and say why.","Find near-duplicate listings (same locality, BHK, floor and an area within a few percent) and remove exact duplicate rows. Build a duplicate group id for later.","Fit OLS of log price on log carpet area and BHK, then add bathrooms, age, city, tier and listing year. Explain why the BHK coefficient changes sign, and compare residual plots for price and log price.","Compare random 5-fold cross-validation with GroupKFold on your duplicate groups for a gradient-boosting model and for OLS. Explain the gap.","Study the missing values in age_years and price. Argue which mechanism (MCAR, MAR or MNAR) fits each, then test one imputation with a missing-indicator against dropping the rows."],"answer_key":"Truth behind the file (synthetic, so these are exact).\nPrice model: ln(price) = city and tier base + locality effect + 0.86 * ln(carpet area) - 0.05 per BHK at fixed area + type effect (villa +0.18, house +0.06, studio -0.05) - 0.011 per year of age up to 25 years (-0.004 after) + 0.04 for RERA + furnishing (semi +0.03, full +0.075) + 0.035 per parking slot + 0.006 per amenity - 0.022 per km to metro + growth of 5 to 9 percent a year depending on the city (a pooled fit gives about 6 percent) + noise with an sd of 0.10 to 0.18 (larger in prime tiers and in Mumbai). About 3 percent of listings carry a luxury uplift.\nAsking premium over the market price: owners about 10 percent, brokers about 7 percent, builders about 2.5 percent. Prices are rounded to attractive numbers.\nArea conversions: built-up = 1.12 x carpet, super built-up = 1.35 x carpet (each with some spread), 1 sq yd = 9 sqft, 1 sq m = 10.76 sqft. Your fitted area elasticity should be about 0.8 to 0.9 once bhk and bathrooms are in the model, and about 0.75 if you leave them out. BHK alone looks positive (about +0.37 per BHK) but is about -0.05 at fixed area.\nMess you should find: 4,923 distinct listing ids and 39 exact duplicate rows; about 500 properties posted 2 or 3 times (about 1,200 rows in such groups); 214 hidden prices (MNAR: about 1 percent of the cheapest third versus 10 percent of the dearest third); 25 prices with a wrong order of magnitude; 21 impossible areas; 88 floors above total floors; about 20 impossible ages; age missing for under-construction homes (structural) and for old homes that hide it (about 3 percent for 0 to 5 years, 7 percent for 6 to 15, 37 percent above 15); floors blank for houses and villas (structural).\nConfounding: new stock sits in cheaper tiers, so the raw correlation between age and log price per sqft is only about -0.05, but it is about -0.3 within a city and tier.\nBenchmarks after cleaning (log price, 5-fold CV): OLS R2 about 0.87 to 0.89, gradient boosting about 0.85 to 0.88. A random split scores about 0.01 to 0.04 R2 higher than a split grouped by property because of the twin listings. Raw rupee prices show a fan-shaped residual plot; log prices do not.","topics":["regression","data cleaning","multicollinearity","heteroscedasticity","duplicates","missing data","feature engineering","validation"],"used_in":["study/analyst/ch07.html","study/stats/ch06.html","study/ml/ch02.html","study/ml/ch03.html","study/ml/ch07.html","study/ds/ch05.html","study/python/ch17.html","project:6","project:66"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_house_prices.py","file_info":[{"path":"house_prices/listings.csv","kb":819},{"path":"house_prices/localities.csv","kb":2}],"size_kb":821,"columns":24},{"id":"support_tickets","title":"Customer support tickets of a fintech app (Hinglish text)","domain":"Customer support","level":"intermediate","files":["support_tickets.csv"],"rows":3327,"summary":"About 3,300 support tickets of a fictional UPI, wallet and cards app called PayNest, written in English, Hinglish (Hindi in Roman letters mixed with English), a little Devanagari Hindi and terse chat style, with typos, SMS spellings, emojis and email signatures. It supports category and escalation models, duplicate detection and resolution-time analysis. Several columns are only filled after a ticket is closed, so careless features leak the answer.","dictionary":[{"col":"ticket_id","type":"id","meaning":"Ticket id (T-xxxxxx)."},{"col":"created_at","type":"datetime","meaning":"When the ticket was created, stored as text in five formats: ISO with +05:30, dd/mm/yyyy hh:mm (IST), epoch seconds (UTC), 'Mar 5, 2024 2:22 PM' (IST) and ISO with Z (UTC)."},{"col":"customer_id","type":"id","meaning":"Customer id. Some customers write several tickets."},{"col":"channel","type":"category","meaning":"email, chat, whatsapp or phone (phone tickets are call transcripts, mostly in lower case)."},{"col":"tier","type":"category","meaning":"Customer plan: Basic, Plus or Premium."},{"col":"subject","type":"text","meaning":"Short subject line. Blank for most chat tickets, and 'Re:' is added on re-sent tickets."},{"col":"body","type":"text","meaning":"The customer's message. Mixed languages, typos, emojis, signatures and sometimes a quoted reply."},{"col":"category","type":"category","meaning":"Issue category set by the agent. About 12 percent are wrong, in fixed confusable pairs."},{"col":"priority","type":"category","meaning":"Priority P1 (highest) to P4 set by the agent. It follows customer tier and issue type more than the wording of the message."},{"col":"assigned_team","type":"category","meaning":"Team the ticket was routed to, derived from the agent's category."},{"col":"escalated_to_l2","type":"bool","meaning":"1 if the ticket was escalated to level-2 support, else 0. A good prediction target."},{"col":"first_response_min","type":"int","meaning":"Minutes until the first reply."},{"col":"resolved_at","type":"datetime","meaning":"When the ticket was closed (IST). Blank if still open at the end of June 2025. Only known after closing."},{"col":"resolution_hours","type":"float","meaning":"Hours from creation to closing. Blank for open tickets, which is censoring. Only known after closing."},{"col":"csat","type":"float","meaning":"Customer satisfaction 1 to 5. Only about 21 percent of customers answer. Only known after closing."},{"col":"reopened","type":"category","meaning":"Y if the ticket was reopened, N if not, blank for open tickets. Only known after closing."},{"col":"resolution_code","type":"category","meaning":"How the ticket was closed (REFUND_INITIATED, ESCALATED_TO_BANK, DUPLICATE_TICKET ...). Only known after closing."},{"col":"closing_note","type":"text","meaning":"The agent's closing note. Only known after closing."},{"col":"merged_into","type":"id","meaning":"Id of an earlier ticket this one was merged into. Filled for only some duplicates."}],"gotchas":["Leakage: resolution_code, closing_note, resolved_at, resolution_hours, csat and reopened are filled only after a ticket is closed, so they cannot be features for a model that works at creation time.","Near-duplicates: about 7 percent of tickets are re-sends by the same customer within two days ('Re:', 'Reminder', 'Third time writing'), and about 110 tickets from about 105 different customers describe the same payment outage on 2024-11-22. Only some are marked in merged_into.","The category label is noisy (agents set about 12 percent wrongly, in fixed pairs such as payment failed and refund status), and priority follows customer tier and issue type more than the wording.","Text is code-mixed: English, Hinglish, a little Devanagari, terse chat, typos, SMS spellings, emojis, signatures and quoted replies. There is no language column, and created_at mixes five formats and two time zones.","Concept drift and censoring: the autopay_mandate category exists only from 2025-03-01, 91 tickets are still open (no resolution), and csat is missing for most tickets in a way that depends on how the ticket went."],"tasks":["Parse created_at into one timezone-aware column (IST). Count how many rows use each of the five formats.","Plot tickets per day and find the outage day. How many tickets arrive between 09:30 and 12:30 IST on 2024-11-22, and what do they say?","Train a TF-IDF plus logistic regression classifier for category on subject and body with 5-fold cross-validation. Look at the confusion matrix and the errors.","Estimate the label noise: read 40 tickets the model gets wrong and say how many look like agent mistakes. Compare with the answer key.","Predict escalated_to_l2 using only columns known at creation time. Then add resolution_code and see what happens. Which columns are leaky and why?","Find near-duplicate tickets (same customer, cosine similarity of TF-IDF above 0.8, within 48 hours). How many are marked in merged_into?","Split by time (train before 2025-03-01, test after) and measure what happens to the autopay_mandate tickets. Suggest what a production system should do.","Compare the csat response rate for slow and fast tickets. Argue why dropping tickets without csat would bias a satisfaction report."],"answer_key":"Truth behind the file (synthetic).\nThe text is generated from the true category; the category column is wrong for about 12 percent of tickets (pairs: payment failed and refund status, charges dispute and fraud report, login locked and KYC, app bug to login locked, autopay to charges dispute), so about 0.88 is the accuracy ceiling for a text model. A TF-IDF logistic regression reaches about 0.88 in 5-fold cross-validation.\nLanguage mix (generator truth): about 50 percent English, 37 percent Hinglish, 3 percent Devanagari Hindi, 10 percent terse chat. About 5 percent of Hinglish tickets end with a Tamil, Telugu, Bengali or Marathi closing phrase.\nEscalation (about 13 percent of tickets) depends on fraud, KYC, charges disputes, amounts over Rs 10,000 and Premium tier. A logistic regression on text, channel, tier and priority gets ROC-AUC of about 0.60; adding resolution_code lifts it to about 0.87, which is leakage.\nNear-duplicates: 217 re-sent tickets (same customer, 2 to 48 hours later); about 45 percent of them carry merged_into. The outage cluster has about 110 extra payment_failed tickets between 09:30 and 12:30 IST on 2024-11-22, about 30 percent merged into the first one.\nOther facts: 91 tickets are open; about 21 percent of tickets have csat, and the response rate is about 38 percent for tickets slower than 72 hours versus about 20 percent for faster ones; autopay_mandate appears only from 2025-03-01.","topics":["text classification","code-mixed text","label leakage","near-duplicates","label noise","concept drift","censoring","feature engineering"],"used_in":["study/genai/ch01.html","study/genai/ch02.html","study/genai/ch08.html","study/ds/ch02.html","study/ds/ch05.html","study/ml/ch08.html","project:33","project:9"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_support_tickets.py","file_info":[{"path":"support_tickets.csv","kb":999}],"size_kb":999,"columns":19},{"id":"product_reviews","title":"Marketplace product reviews (English, Hinglish, emoji)","domain":"E-commerce / NLP","level":"intermediate","files":["product_reviews/reviews.csv","product_reviews/products.csv"],"rows":4669,"rows_by_file":{"reviews.csv":4669,"products.csv":120},"summary":"About 4,700 reviews of 120 fictional products on an Indian online marketplace, written in English, Hinglish (Hindi in Roman letters mixed with English), some Devanagari Hindi and SMS spellings, with emojis, sarcasm and encoding damage. Star ratings, words and a small human sentiment label do not always agree, and some reviews are fake. It is a good place to practise text cleaning, sentiment models and honest error analysis.","dictionary":[{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"review_id","type":"id","meaning":"Review id (R-xxxxxx)."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"product_id","type":"id","meaning":"Product reviewed, joins to products.csv."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"reviewer_id","type":"id","meaning":"Reviewer id (U-xxxxx)."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"review_date","type":"datetime","meaning":"Date of the review, stored as text in four formats (2024-05-12, 12/05/2024, May 12, 2024, 12-05-24)."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"rating","type":"int","meaning":"Star rating 1 to 5. In some rows it is text such as '5 stars'."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"title","type":"text","meaning":"Review title (the first part of the text)."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"review_text","type":"text","meaning":"The review. Mixed languages, typos, emojis, HTML entities (&amp;), a few zero-width characters, line breaks and a few mojibake rows."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"verified_purchase","type":"bool","meaning":"Whether the buyer bought the product, written as Y/N or True/False."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"helpful_votes","type":"int","meaning":"Helpful votes. In about a quarter of rows it is text such as '3 people found this helpful'."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"device","type":"category","meaning":"app or web."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"seller_replied","type":"category","meaning":"Y if the seller replied. Mostly low-star reviews, replied to after the fact."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"order_to_review_days","type":"int","meaning":"Days between delivery and the review."},{"file":"reviews.csv","col":"sentiment_label","type":"category","meaning":"Human label pos, neu or neg, present for about 30 percent of rows, with annotator mistakes."},{"file":"products.csv","col":"product_id","type":"id","meaning":"Product id."},{"file":"products.csv","col":"name","type":"string","meaning":"Fictional brand and product name."},{"file":"products.csv","col":"category","type":"category","meaning":"Product category."},{"file":"products.csv","col":"price_inr","type":"int","meaning":"List price in INR."},{"file":"products.csv","col":"launch_date","type":"datetime","meaning":"Date the product was listed."},{"file":"products.csv","col":"seller_id","type":"id","meaning":"Seller id."}],"gotchas":["Stars and words disagree: about 3 to 4 percent of reviews have mis-clicked stars (such as 'worst, waste of money' with 5 stars), and about 2 percent are sarcastic (positive words, negative meaning).","About 6 percent of reviews are fake: 25 reviewer ids post generic 5-star praise on 8 products in three bursts, none verified.","Text is messy: English, Hinglish, Devanagari, SMS spellings, emojis (some contradict the words), HTML entities, zero-width characters, a few mojibake rows. Many short reviews share the same wording, so identical text alone does not prove copying.","Wrong types and formats: some ratings are text ('5 stars'), helpful_votes is partly text, dates come in four formats, and verified_purchase mixes Y/N with True/False.","Leakage and drift: seller_replied is filled mostly for low-star reviews after the fact, and 6 products lose quality from 2024-06-01, so a time-based split is harder than a random one."],"tasks":["Load both files and fix the types: parse rating and helpful_votes into numbers, parse review_date, and make verified_purchase boolean. Plot the rating distribution.","Clean the text: unescape HTML entities, strip zero-width characters and fix the mojibake rows (hint: encode as latin-1 and decode as UTF-8). Count how many rows each fix touches.","Build a TF-IDF plus logistic regression model that predicts negative, neutral or positive (stars 1-2, 3, 4-5) from review_text. Report accuracy and a confusion matrix.","Read 40 errors of your model. How many are sarcasm, mis-clicked stars or mixed-language text? Compare with the answer key.","Find the fake-review bursts using reviewer_id, verified_purchase, rating, date and text similarity. How many reviews and products are affected, and how does removing them change the average rating?","Add seller_replied and helpful_votes to the model and see how the scores change. Explain why this is leakage.","Compare a random split with a time-based split (train before 2024-06-01). Check the 6 products whose quality dropped."],"answer_key":"Truth behind the file (synthetic).\nEach product has a hidden quality q (sd 0.6). P(negative) = sigmoid(-1.55 - 1.1 * q) before sarcasm is added. In the file about 21 percent of reviews are negative (sarcastic ones included), about 10 percent neutral and about 69 percent positive. Stars given the sentiment: positive 5/4/3 = 68/27/5 percent, neutral 3/2/4 = 55/15/30, negative 1/2/3 = 58/30/12. About 47 percent of ratings are 5 stars.\nPlanted problems: about 3.6 percent mis-clicked stars (a negative review with 5 stars or a positive one with 1 star); about 2.2 percent of reviews are sarcastic (positive words, negative sentiment, stars 1 to 3); 268 fake reviews from reviewer ids U-15000 to U-15024 on 8 products, all 5 stars, unverified, in three bursts; 68 exact repeated rows and 43 texts pasted across products; about 1.5 percent of rows with non-ASCII text are mojibake; about 2 percent of ratings are text; the human sentiment label covers about 30 percent of rows and is wrong for about 6 percent of them.\nLanguage mix: about 56 percent English, 28 percent Hinglish, 4 percent Devanagari, 12 percent SMS spellings.\nBenchmarks: TF-IDF plus logistic regression predicts the three-class sentiment from stars with about 0.85 accuracy and the exact star rating with about 0.62 accuracy (5-fold cross-validation). seller_replied is Y for about 45 percent of 1 and 2 star reviews and about 3 percent of 4 and 5 star reviews.","topics":["sentiment analysis","code-mixed text","label noise","fake reviews","text cleaning","mojibake","data leakage","error analysis"],"used_in":["study/genai/ch01.html","study/genai/ch02.html","study/genai/ch08.html","study/genai/ch10.html","study/dl/ch10.html","study/ml/ch04.html","study/ml/ch12.html","project:9","project:34","project:53"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_product_reviews.py","file_info":[{"path":"product_reviews/reviews.csv","kb":800},{"path":"product_reviews/products.csv","kb":8}],"size_kb":808,"columns":13},{"id":"movie_ratings","title":"Streaming film ratings (Indian and international films)","domain":"Recommender systems","level":"intermediate","files":["movie_ratings/ratings.csv","movie_ratings/movies.csv","movie_ratings/users.csv"],"rows":41085,"rows_by_file":{"ratings.csv":41085,"movies.csv":950,"users.csv":1303},"summary":"About 41,000 star ratings from 1,300 users on 950 films in a fictional streaming app, in a MovieLens-like layout of three tables. All film titles are invented, so any match with a real film is a coincidence. The data has the usual recommender traps: a few films get most ratings, many users have almost none, bots and a review bomb are mixed in, and the rating scale changes part-way.","dictionary":[{"file":"ratings.csv","col":"user_id","type":"id","meaning":"User id. Mostly a number, but about 2 percent of rows write it as text with a u prefix (u0042)."},{"file":"ratings.csv","col":"movie_id","type":"id","meaning":"Film id, joins to movies.csv."},{"file":"ratings.csv","col":"rating","type":"float","meaning":"Star rating. Whole stars (1 to 5) before 2023-06-01 and half stars (0.5 to 5) after. A few values are impossible (0, 6, -1, 10) or text ('five', '4/5', 'N/A')."},{"file":"ratings.csv","col":"timestamp","type":"int","meaning":"When the rating was made, Unix time in UTC. Mostly seconds, but about 6 percent of rows are in milliseconds (13 digits)."},{"file":"movies.csv","col":"movie_id","type":"id","meaning":"Film id."},{"file":"movies.csv","col":"title","type":"string","meaning":"Invented title with the release year in brackets. Titles come from a small word list, so about half carry a number (2, 3 ...) to tell repeats apart."},{"file":"movies.csv","col":"genres","type":"string","meaning":"One to three genres separated by a pipe, for example Drama|Romance."},{"file":"movies.csv","col":"language","type":"category","meaning":"Main language of the film."},{"file":"movies.csv","col":"release_year","type":"int","meaning":"Release year, 1990 to 2024."},{"file":"users.csv","col":"user_id","type":"id","meaning":"User id. The last three ids are bot accounts."},{"file":"users.csv","col":"age_band","type":"category","meaning":"Age group. Spelling varies ('18 - 24', '25to34') and some are blank."},{"file":"users.csv","col":"gender","type":"category","meaning":"Gender with inconsistent codes (M, F, Male, female, male, Other, Non-binary) and blanks."},{"file":"users.csv","col":"city","type":"category","meaning":"City. Bangalore and Bombay appear as extra spellings."},{"file":"users.csv","col":"signup_date","type":"datetime","meaning":"Date the account was created."},{"file":"users.csv","col":"preferred_language","type":"category","meaning":"Language the user watches most."}],"gotchas":["Three bot accounts rate about 130 films each with 5.0 inside a few minutes, and one film receives a burst of 0.5 ratings within a single hour (review bombing).","The rating scale changes on 2023-06-01 (whole stars before, half stars after), and timestamps mix seconds and milliseconds, so averages and time-based splits need care.","Ratings contain impossible values and text, user ids with a u prefix, repeated user and film pairs (people who re-rated), and inconsistent gender, age and city spellings.","Popularity bias and cold start: a few films collect most of the ratings, so a 'most popular' recommender looks strong. Many users have 3 or fewer ratings, and 60 films are released late with little history.","People rate what they expect to like, so the observed mean rating is higher than the true average taste over all user and film pairs (missing not at random)."],"tasks":["Load the three files. Convert timestamps to datetimes (check the digit count) and user ids to integers, and report how many rows each fix touches.","Clean ratings: keep only values from 0.5 to 5, drop text values, and keep the latest rating for each user and film pair. Plot the rating distribution before and after 2023-06-01.","Find the bot accounts and the review-bombed film using ratings per user, rating variance and ratings per hour. Decide what to remove.","Plot ratings per film (rank versus count) and ratings per user. What share of users have 3 or fewer ratings, and what share of ratings go to the top 10 percent of films?","Build a leave-last-out split in time (hold out each user's latest rating). Score a 'most popular' recommender with HR@10.","Add an item-item cosine recommender on the same split and compare HR@10 with popularity, overall and for users with 5 to 10 ratings versus 50 or more.","Compare a random hold-out with the time-based split for the same models and explain the difference. Then explain why the observed average rating is higher than the true average taste."],"answer_key":"Truth behind the file (synthetic).\nModel: rating = 3.45 + user bias (sd 0.4) + film bias (sd 0.45) + 1.1 * (taste of the user . taste of the film, 5 hidden dimensions) + a language bonus (+0.45 for the user's preferred language, +0.15 for English) + noise (sd 0.65), then rounded to whole stars before 2023-06-01 and half stars after.\nExposure is missing not at random: a user picks films with probability proportional to popularity (Zipf, rank to the power -0.85) times exp(0.9 * taste), so people rate what they expect to like. The mean over all user and film pairs is about 3.4, while the observed mean rating is about 3.9.\nPlanted problems: bot user ids 1301, 1302 and 1303 (about 130 ratings each, all 5.0, a few minutes apart); a review bomb of about 150 ratings of 0.5 on one film (id in the generator truth) on 2024-12-26 between 19:00 and 20:00 UTC; 2,499 timestamps in milliseconds; 790 user ids with a u prefix; 185 numeric ratings outside 0.5 to 5 and 39 text ratings; about 650 repeated user and film pairs (re-ratings with a later timestamp).\nCold start: about 12 percent of users get only 1 to 3 ratings, about 7 percent are new users in the last month, and 60 films are released between late September and December 2024.\nBenchmarks on a leave-last-out split with the bots removed (about 1,070 users with 5 or more ratings): a random recommender has HR@10 of about 0.011, the most-popular recommender about 0.20, and item-item cosine about 0.24 (it helps most for active users).","topics":["recommender systems","popularity bias","cold start","missing not at random","data cleaning","anomaly detection","evaluation","time-based split"],"used_in":["study/ds/ch10.html","study/ml/ch09.html","study/ml/ch12.html","study/ml/ch13.html","study/math/ch09.html","project:27"],"generator":"tools/datasets/make_movie_ratings.py","file_info":[{"path":"movie_ratings/ratings.csv","kb":933},{"path":"movie_ratings/movies.csv","kb":49},{"path":"movie_ratings/users.csv","kb":50}],"size_kb":1033,"columns":4}];
